"""Rules for telling a market-driven selloff from a company in trouble (a local addition).

In a falling, frightened market good and bad companies are sold together. Fear and forced selling can push a sound
company's price below what its business is worth, and they can also hide a company that is in real trouble. These
rules give the debaters and the two managers one way to tell which it is, from evidence about the company and not
from the price: the market-context tool already says how much of a stock's decline the market explains, and the
fundamentals tools give the balance-sheet facts for a survival test.

The rules do not make a lower price a reason to buy. A company that fails the survival test, or whose decline is
mostly its own, is treated as having earned its price.

Set ``TRADINGAGENTS_NO_SELLOFF_RULES=1`` to leave the rules out of every prompt, so the same ticker can be run with
and without them and the ratings compared.
"""

import os

DISABLE_ENV = "TRADINGAGENTS_NO_SELLOFF_RULES"

CORE = """

## Selloffs and deals

A market in a pullback, correction or bear market (the market report's market-context section gives the label and the VIX percentile) sells good and bad companies together. Fear can push a sound company's price below what its business is worth; it can also hide a company in real trouble. Tell which from evidence about the company, not from the price.

1. Weakness the market explains is not evidence against the company. A decline, high volatility, a high beta, or weak technical readings (below moving averages, low RSI, a downtrend) count against the stock only to the extent the market does not explain them. The market report's market-context section says what share of the stock's recent decline is market-implied and what share is not (its "Reading" lines). Use those figures; if the reports do not give them, say "not established". The same goes for weakness the stock's own sector explains: when the section also gives sector-fund lines (its "Sector reading" lines), a decline the sector fund explains is a sector-wide selloff, the market's panic in one part of it, and it counts against the stock no more than a decline the S&P 500 explains. The part of a fall the S&P 500 alone does not explain is not the company's own if the sector explains it.
2. Evidence about the business counts in both directions, by its size, and by the same standard in both. What counts against a company is company-specific evidence that the business got worse: estimates or guidance cut, results that missed, a falling margin or a drop in operating cash flow, distress or financing filings, dilution, lost customers or contracts, a weaker balance sheet. What counts for it is the mirror image: estimates or guidance raised, results that beat, a rising margin or a rise in operating cash flow, falling debt, a shrinking share count, new customers or contracts, a stronger balance sheet. Weigh each item by its size and net it against the opposite evidence: the scorecard's evidence tally (line I) gives the sizes with fixed thresholds, and a small move it names as a "watch item" (a one-quarter margin dip of a point or two, a few estimate cuts among many raises) is neither deterioration nor improvement; one quarter does not break a trend of several. A change in debt counts, in either direction, only when cash does not cover the debt: debt that doubled while cash is many times larger is not a weaker balance sheet. Three things are signals, not evidence, until something else confirms them: insider selling (nearly every large company has it; the insider report marks a sale made under a pre-arranged Rule 10b5-1 plan, which was set long before and tells you least, and when a report gives no such mark it cannot say whether a sale was planned), a cluster of insider buying (the insider report's "Cluster" line: three or more different insiders buying on the open market within 30 days; a purchase is the rarer event and the mirror image of a sale), and a filing whose content the reports do not give. Name each with its figure as "unresolved". Selling lets you argue for a smaller or staged position and a buying cluster for a larger or earlier first step, but do not count either as deterioration or as improvement, and neither alone moves a rating. A planned sale and a sale that follows a vesting or an option exercise carry no weight on their own, and no insider trades at all is neutral. A single open-market purchase is the insider's own choice with their own money, so it carries some weight, graded by the share it added to what the buyer already owned (the report's "Purchase size" line and its "Change that day" column): a large one (10% or more) is a larger signal, a modest one (1% to 10%) a smaller one, and a token one (under 1%) is noted and nothing more; dollar size alone is not the measure, and a purchase made in a batch (see the Cluster note) or reversed by a later sale carries none. Such a purchase is still a signal, not evidence: it can argue for a larger or earlier first step and counts for more when evidence about the business points the same way, but alone it moves no rating. Insider signals also age: the information in an insider purchase is mostly in the price within weeks to a few months, and it is strongest in smaller companies, so a large company's insider trades carry less. Count age from the trade date, as the insider report's "days ago" figures give it. A purchase in the last 60 days is the live signal; one 60 to 180 days old is context only and is not called recent; the report's window ends at 180 days, and nothing older is used. A cluster counts only when the report's Cluster line names one that ended within the last 90 days, and a cluster that the report's "Cluster note" calls likely routine (many insiders buying at exactly one price on the same day, as a scheduled purchase program does) is not a cluster: it carries no weight and is not named as a signal. The newest unplanned action sets the direction, and the report's "Latest move" line names it: a sale that follows the same insider's own purchase reverses that purchase (the "Sold after buying" line names them, and their purchases are left out of the Cluster line), so it no longer counts as buying; a later sale by a different insider is a separate decision that weakens earlier buying without cancelling it; a sale the report marks as planned does not change the direction. The report's dollar total over a window ignores order, so take the direction from the Latest move line, not from that total. The exception is a filing that shows the company withheld or delayed what it must report (a late-filing notice, non-reliance on its financial statements, a notice of delisting, a bankruptcy filing): that counts against it whatever else the filing says. Missing information is of three kinds, treated differently: what the company withheld (the exception just named; scorecard line J lists it) counts against it; what we could not see (a data tool failed or has no coverage; line J calls it our data gap) counts neither for nor against the company, but it blocks a deal call and argues for a smaller, staged position, so say "not established: data gap"; and what this company never reports is neutral.
3. Survival test. A company can be called a deal only if it can get through a year of bad markets without a damaging financing: cash and equivalents cover at least 12 months of cash burn (or free cash flow is positive); no going-concern warning; no large debt due without funding in sight; no run of equity sales, registrations or a fast-rising share count. A company that fails is not a deal. Cheap does not rescue it. A company whose cash covers all its debt and whose free cash flow is positive passes on the numbers (scorecard line H says so): nothing can fall due that cash cannot pay, so unread going-concern language or an unread debt-maturity schedule is not a gap that blocks a deal call; a filing tool that cannot read a foreign company's 20-F or 6-K is our gap, not the company's. When cash does not cover the debt, or is unknown, the maturities and any going-concern language must be read, and unread they are a data gap.
4. A deal needs all five: (a) the market is in a drawdown (pullback or worse) or the VIX is in its top tenth of the past year; (b) the stock's decline is mostly market-driven: the market explains 60% or more of it over the 63 sessions and at least 45% over the last 21 (take both figures from the market-context "Reading" lines; do not pick the window that suits the conclusion. If the section also gives sector-fund lines, the stock's sector fund may explain the fall instead, by the same two bars: use both windows from the S&P 500 "Reading" lines or both from the "Sector reading" lines, never one of each. A sector explanation counts for (b) only: the company evidence of (c) and the survival test of (d) are judged exactly as before); (c) no company-specific deterioration: the estimate, results, margin, cash-flow and balance-sheet data in the reports show none (rule 2; unresolved signals do not fail it, deterioration evidence does); (d) it passes the survival test; (e) the valuation is low against its own history or its estimates, not stretched (growth counts, in both directions: a premium to its peers that expected growth pays for, a growth-adjusted P/E (PEG) of 1.0 or less on scorecard line H or I, is not stretched, and a multiple that growth does not pay for, a PEG of 2.0 or more, is; a PEG of 1.0 or less also pays for an earnings yield up to 2 points below the 10-year Treasury yield; the PEG rests on the same consensus growth the estimates test, so say so). A condition the reports do not establish counts as not met: for (b) that means figures that are missing, and for (c) estimate, results or cash-flow data that are missing. The scorecard's rule check (line H) is the code's reading of each condition with fixed thresholds, worked out before the debate: start from it, overturn a result only with a specific figure from the reports (name the figure), and do not move a threshold to suit a conclusion. A condition the code marks UNKNOWN is a gap in our data, not a finding about the company, and it still counts as not met for a deal call.
5. If all five hold, call it a market-driven selloff and a deal. The price decline and the volatility then do not lower the rating; rate the business and its forecasts, and the rating may be one step more positive than the same facts would earn in a calm market. Advise staged entry (for example in thirds), because volatility will stay high. If any condition fails, name it and do not treat the stock as a deal. A condition that fails or is not established withholds that extra step and nothing more: it lowers nothing. A stock that is not a deal is rated like any other stock in a calm market, by the evidence about its business (scorecard line I), so do not hold a stock at Hold for want of a deal when its evidence earns more, and do not lower one for it. If the decline is mostly the stock's own, or the survival test fails, the weakness is earned.
6. Never call a deal on a lower price alone, and never treat a stock as safe because the market is also down."""

ROLE_LINES = {
    "bull": "If you argue this stock is a deal, show the five conditions with figures from the reports; a condition you skip is a point conceded to the bear. If you cite insider buying, give the cluster line's figures and call it an unresolved signal (rule 2); say whether the Latest move line shows a later sale, and do not cite a cluster that the Cluster note calls likely routine. Cite a lone purchase by its grade on the Purchase size line, never as a cluster.",
    "bear": "Do not argue against the company from a price decline, volatility, beta or weak technicals that the market or its sector explains. Argue from company-specific evidence and the survival test. If the market or its sector explains the decline and the company passes the survival test, concede that and argue valuation or fundamentals instead. If you cite insider selling, say whether the insider report marks it as a planned sale (Rule 10b5-1): a planned sale carries little weight (rule 2). Say too whether a sale follows the same insider's own purchase (the Sold after buying line): that reverses the purchase.",
    "aggressive": "An aggressive case for buying a selloff must clear the survival test and the other four conditions first; say how each is met.",
    "conservative": "Market-wide or sector-wide volatility and a decline the market or its sector explains are not risks of this company. Argue from company-specific evidence and the survival test; say so if the company passes them.",
    "neutral": "Separate what the market and the stock's sector explain from what is the company's own, and judge the company on the second.",
    "research_manager": "Open your Rationale with a **Selloff diagnosis** of two to four sentences: the market state, the share of the stock's decline the market explains (both windows, and the sector fund's share when the reports give it), any company-specific deterioration and any unresolved signals (rule 2), the survival-test result, and whether all five conditions of rule 4 hold (name any the debate did not establish; that condition counts as not met). Then give the arguments that decided it. Weigh the bull and bear cases by rules 1 to 6: a bear argument that rests only on price weakness the market or its sector explains carries no weight, and a bull argument that skips the survival test carries none either. A condition that fails or is not established withholds the deal step only; it is not a reason to rate below what the business's own evidence earns in a calm market.",
    "portfolio_manager": "Open your Investment Thesis with a **Selloff diagnosis** of two to four sentences in the same form: the market state, the share of the decline the market explains (both windows, and the sector fund's share when the reports give it), company-specific deterioration and any unresolved signals, the survival-test result, and whether the five conditions hold. Check the Research Manager's diagnosis against the risk debate. A conservative argument that rests on market-wide or sector-wide volatility alone does not count against the stock, and an aggressive argument for buying must clear the survival test. A condition that fails or is not established withholds the deal step only; it is not a reason to rate below what the business's own evidence earns in a calm market.",
}


def enabled() -> bool:
    return os.getenv(DISABLE_ENV, "").strip().lower() not in ("1", "true", "yes", "on")


def selloff_rules(role: str) -> str:
    """The rules plus the line for ``role``, or an empty string when the rules are switched off."""
    if not enabled():
        return ""
    return CORE + "\n\n" + ROLE_LINES[role] + "\n"
